=== insert/on-conflict-do-update [mysql+unfaithful-upsert]
sql:  "INSERT INTO users (email, name) VALUES (?, ?) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE name = ?, updated_at = ?, visits = ?"
args: [string("user@example.com"), string("Ann"), string("Ann"), core.Raw("NOW()"), int(2)]

=== insert/on-conflict-do-update [mysql]
err:  unsupported "mysql does not support ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE; MySQL's ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE fires on any unique index rather than the conflict target you named, so the translation is not faithful; set databases.<name>.allow_unfaithful_upsert: true (or MySQLDialect.AllowUnfaithfulUpsert, when you build the dialect yourself) if the table has exactly one unique constraint, or do the read-then-write explicitly in a transaction"

=== insert/on-conflict-do-update [postgres]
sql:  "INSERT INTO users (email, name) VALUES (?, ?) ON CONFLICT (email) DO UPDATE SET name = ?, updated_at = ?, visits = ?"
args: [string("user@example.com"), string("Ann"), string("Ann"), core.Raw("NOW()"), int(2)]

